{
  "id": "kldi0nx",
  "title": "Chapter 10: Acids, Bases and Salts",
  "order": 59,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o4ho14q",
      "content": "Acids, bases and salts are important classes of chemical compounds that play a crucial role in chemistry and daily life. These substances participate in numerous chemical reactions and are widely used in industries, laboratories and households."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "pH Range",
          "value": "0 to 14",
          "icon": "clock"
        },
        {
          "label": "Neutral pH",
          "value": "7.0",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Acidic pH Range",
          "value": "< 7.0",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Basic pH Range",
          "value": "> 7.0",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "hy6vujk"
    },
    {
      "id": "g9gz23o",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Acids"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Acids release hydrogen ions (H+) in aqueous solutions and turn blue litmus paper red.",
        "Bases release hydroxide ions (OH-) in aqueous solutions, feel soapy to touch, and turn red litmus paper blue.",
        "Alkalis are bases that dissolve completely in water, such as sodium hydroxide (NaOH) and potassium hydroxide (KOH).",
        "The pH scale quantitatively measures hydrogen ion concentration from 0 (strongly acidic) to 14 (strongly basic).",
        "Neutralization is a chemical process where an acid reacts with a base to produce salt and water."
      ],
      "id": "3m59vi2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1nf1m94",
      "content": "• Acids are substances that produce hydrogen ions (H⁺) when dissolved in water."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9cwchzw",
      "content": "• They have a sour taste."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5wbufy3",
      "content": "• They turn blue litmus paper red."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0jpi38p",
      "content": "• They react with metals to produce hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4a5pkuc",
      "content": "Examples of common acids:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "dut581j",
      "content": "• Hydrochloric acid (HCl)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gc59ilz",
      "content": "• Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5vpbi31",
      "content": "• Nitric acid (HNO₃)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "oiccc3g",
      "content": "• Acetic acid (CH₃COOH)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ilyaowm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Bases"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kcdha0s",
      "content": "• Bases are substances that produce hydroxide ions (OH⁻) in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9vbb1cf",
      "content": "• They taste bitter and feel slippery."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7jpsjww",
      "content": "• They turn red litmus paper blue."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q47ah8f",
      "content": "Examples of bases:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "de57but",
      "content": "• Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "tssglc8",
      "content": "• Potassium hydroxide (KOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ekhg3rm",
      "content": "• Calcium hydroxide Ca(OH)₂"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "A neutralization reaction is a chemical reaction in which an acid and a base react quantitatively with each other to form salt and water.",
      "id": "8313hcq"
    },
    {
      "id": "ut1x0s2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Alkalis"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ch3hu3t",
      "content": "Alkalis are bases that dissolve in water and produce hydroxide ions. All alkalis are bases but all bases are not alkalis."
    },
    {
      "id": "9x249z3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Properties of Acids and Bases"
    },
